.TH CCOL_LOG_FATAL 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
clog_fatal \- emit a log line at FATAL level with a backtrace, then terminate the process
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clogger.h>

clog_fatal(clog lg, const char *fmt, ...);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Writes a structured log line at CLOG_FATAL severity, appends a full
stack backtrace, and then stops the calling process with a call to
exit(EXIT_FAILURE).  This macro never returns to the caller.

exit() runs the exit drain of the library (see clog_open_fd_mp(3)),
which delivers what every other async logger still holds and lets
every gzip compression that a rotation of any logger in the process
has queued finish, including the one that the fatal record itself can
cause.  So no rotated file is left as a half-written \&.gz file beside
its source.  One budget of 5 seconds bounds that drain, and the drain
takes the lock of a logger only when that logger has a queue or a
compression to drain.  A thread of another logger that blocks for
ever (inside a write to a pipe that nobody reads, for example) thus
never keeps the process from stopping.

The same budget, counted from the start of the first fatal call of the
process, bounds the fatal record itself.  On an async logger the drain
of its own queue and batch ends by then, and so does every write of the
record to an output that is not a regular file (a pipe, a FIFO, a
socket, a terminal).  An output that takes nothing more in that time,
such as a full pipe whose reader stopped reading, loses the record: the
library writes one line to stderr that says so, unless stderr is that
same output, and the process stops anyway.  A write to a regular file
waits on the disk and not on a reader, and keeps no bound.  The exit
drain that follows shares the deadline, so one budget bounds the whole
stop.  While a fatal call waits for the logger, the writer thread of an
async logger takes on no new work; that wait may run up to 100
milliseconds past the budget, so that a write in progress on the logger
can end and hand the logger over.

Unlike every other log_* macro, clog_fatal goes past the min_level
filter of the logger.  The library always writes the log line and the
backtrace, whatever the current minimum level of the logger is
(CLOG_OFF included), so nothing silently suppresses the cause of the
stop.

The library writes the log line and the backtrace inside the shared
write lock, so they always appear together in the output, even when
other threads log at the same time.

In CLOG_FMT_JSON mode the library embeds the backtrace as an inline
"bt" array inside the JSON object, as clog_error and clog_alert
do.  In logfmt mode it writes the backtrace after the main record, as
continuation lines indented by a tab.  In CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG mode it
writes each frame as a separate syslog message that carries the same
PRI, TIMESTAMP and MSGID as the main record.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
lg     Logger handle (clog).
fmt    printf-style format string.
\&...    Format arguments (optional).
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
A backtrace needs the binary to be linked with -rdynamic.  The capture
itself can fail, on a platform with no execinfo.h or on a transient
allocation failure while the library resolves symbol names.  The
process still stops, and the logger writes one "backtrace unavailable"
marker line in place of the usual output for each frame (a marker
record in CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG mode), so the omission is visible instead of
silent.
When memory pressure lasts for a time, or the content of a record
leaves little room before the internal buffer cap, a single backtrace
frame that the library cannot format is omitted in the same way, while
a shorter frame that follows it can still go out.  The library never
writes an incomplete frame, so every line that it writes is a
complete, well-formed record.  When not one frame fits, the same
"backtrace unavailable" marker takes their place.  Under such pressure
in CLOG_FMT_JSON mode the library can fail to embed the backtrace at
all, and a "bt_error":"unavailable" key then takes the place of "bt",
so the omission is visible and not silent.

On an async logger, clog_fatal first drains everything that
earlier calls already queued, so a message that the application logged
moments before the fatal one reaches the log instead of dying in the
queue.  The macro then writes the fatal record itself synchronously.
Nothing on that path waits without a bound: the library skips a drain
request that the queue cannot accept instead of waiting on it.  The
batch of the writer thread can hold bytes that an earlier write could
not deliver (see clog_open_fd_mp(3)).  Those bytes get one bounded
attempt at delivery, after which the library gives up on them: it
repairs the framing that a split record left open and writes a "log
record truncated: undelivered batch dropped (records=<n> bytes=<m>):
the process stopped" record for the whole records that it gives up on,
ahead of the fatal record.  It does not hold up the stop on a
descriptor that will not take them.

The fatal record is written on the calling thread.  When the logger
writes to a pipe whose reader is gone, that write fails with EPIPE and
the process still ends through exit(EXIT_FAILURE): under the default
CLOG_SIGPIPE_AUTO policy the library first sets SIGPIPE to SIG_IGN if
the disposition is SIG_DFL at that point.  Under CLOG_SIGPIPE_UNTOUCHED
the disposition of the application decides.  See
clog_set_sigpipe_policy(3).

Because clog_fatal terminates the process immediately, any logger
handles that have not been closed are not explicitly freed.  This is
intentional: on program exit the OS reclaims all resources.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
if (!recovered)
    clog_fatal(lg, "unrecoverable state: %s", reason);  /* exits */

if (balance < 0)
    clog_fatal(lg, "invariant violated at step %d", step);  /* exits */
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clog_alert (3),
.BR clog_error (3),
.BR clog_open_fd_mp (3),
.BR clog_open_file_mp (3),
.BR clog_set_sigpipe_policy (3)
